# Cloudflare Pages header rules.
#
# Pages serves HTML with `max-age=0, must-revalidate` but every other asset with
# `max-age=14400` — four hours. That default is right for a marketing site and
# wrong for the console: dev-tools.html and alerts.html update the moment a
# deploy lands, while the script that holds their actual logic does not, so for
# up to four hours a freshly-deployed page runs against stale detectors. That is
# how a fixed false-positive kept being reported after the fix shipped.
#
# max-age=0 does not mean "don't cache" — the browser still stores the file and
# still gets a 304 on almost every load. It only means it must ask first, which
# is one conditional request against a 20KB file, and buys the guarantee that
# what the page is running is what was deployed.

/alerts.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Same reasoning, milder stakes: a new page against four-hour-old styling looks
# broken in a way that reads as a bug in the page.
/style.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
